        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "The stone went darker after treatment. Is that normal?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Either the product was a color enhancer, or the stone has not finished curing. Standard impregnators can make marble look slightly darker for a day or two while the carrier evaporates. If the darkness remains after the full cure time, an enhancing ingredient or heavy application is likely the cause, and the effect is generally permanent on porous stone."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should a stain be removed before the stone is treated?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "You can, but you should not. Sealing locks the stain in place and makes it much harder to remove. Poultice the stain first using a product matched to it: an oil-lifting poultice for grease, or a hydrogen peroxide poultice for organic stains like coffee on light marble. Let the stone dry fully afterward, then seal."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "My marble is hazy after sealing. How do I fix it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Haze is excess sealer that dried on the surface. Apply a small amount of the same sealer to a clean cloth, rub the hazy area for a minute to re-dissolve the residue, then buff completely dry. For stubborn haze, the sealer manufacturer usually names a solvent that removes it. Avoid abrasive pads, which will dull polished marble."
          }
        },
