            1. The 6 Best Marble Sealers, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We ranked these using each maker's technical data sheet, the stone types and finishes the label approves, stated reapplication intervals, and consistent themes in owner feedback about staining after sealing. Products earned priority for strong oil repellency, no change to the finish of polished marble, and forgiving application for a homeowner.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="etch-vs-stain" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Etching and Staining Are Different Problems
          </h2>

          <p>
            Marble is mostly calcite, a form of calcium carbonate. Acids react with calcite and dissolve a microscopic layer of the surface. On polished marble that leaves a dull, slightly rough mark, an etch. Citrus, vinegar, wine, tomato sauce, soda, many fruit juices, and acidic cleaners all etch. The mark is physical damage, not something that has soaked in, and it cannot be wiped away.
          </p>

          <p>
            A stain is the opposite: a substance enters the pores of the stone and leaves color behind. Oil darkens marble, coffee and tea leave brown marks, and red wine leaves pink or purple marks. Stains are what sealers stop. A well-sealed counter gives you minutes to hours to wipe a spill before color penetrates, depending on the substance and the sealer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Red wine on marble often does both at once: it is acidic and it carries pigment. Sealer helps with the color, but the acid still etches. That is why a sealed counter can still show a dull ring where a glass sat overnight, and why owners sometimes think their sealer failed when it actually did the job it can do.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical takeaway is to treat the two problems separately. Use a quality impregnator to block stains. Manage etching with coasters, trivets, cutting boards and quick wipe-ups of anything acidic, and keep a marble polishing compound on hand for light etches on polished surfaces.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="finish-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Polished, Honed or Leathered: Why Finish Changes Everything
          </h2>

          <p>
            Polished marble has a glassy, reflective surface produced by grinding down to very fine abrasives. It is relatively dense at the surface, which helps resist stains, but etches show dramatically against the shine. A polished white marble counter in a busy kitchen will collect etch marks; the question is only how many you are willing to live with.
          </p>

          <p>
            Honed marble is ground to a smooth matte finish without the final polish. It is more absorbent than polished, so it stains more readily and benefits from two coats of sealer. The upside is that etches blend in: the surface is already matte, so a dull spot is far less visible. Many designers recommend honed marble for kitchens for exactly this reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leathered and brushed finishes have a textured, slightly undulating surface. They hide etching even better than honed and feel pleasant to the touch, but the texture traps dirt and absorbs sealer unevenly. Apply with a soft brush to reach into the texture, then wipe excess with a cloth so it does not pool in the low spots.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tumbled marble, common on backsplashes and floors, is the most porous and has rounded, pitted edges. It usually takes two or three coats of sealer and benefits from a color enhancer if you want it to look richer. For the grout between tumbled tiles, our <a href="/best-grout-sealer/">grout sealer guide</a> explains which products seal both at once.
          </p>

